    "body": "> > > Thanks for the suggestions @developer-guy do you have any suggestions for what files we could trigger this module on other than `Chart.yaml`?\r\n> > > For anyone interested in implementing this probably best to use `helm version --client --short` to get the version so it works with helm v2 and v3.\r\n> > \r\n> > \r\n> > thanks @andytom, Helm Chart is basically a structured folder format and looks like following:\r\n> > ![Screen Shot 2020-07-07 at 23 01 58](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/16693043/86836245-e574df00-c0a5-11ea-9bc6-7ac95c6f8af6.png)\r\n> > maybe we can use templates/ folder, NOTES.txt, values.yaml files etc. to trigger this module.\r\n> \r\n> I'm quite familiar with the helm directory layout have been doing a lot of work using helm recently 😀. I was thinking more about things like `helmfile.yaml` from [roboll/helmfile](https://github.com/roboll/helmfile) which would be a good indication that someone is using helm, `templates/` folder, `NOTES.txt` and `values.yaml` might be a bit too generic and trigger too many false positives.\r\n\r\noh sorry, @andytom 😅  , you might be right but there is no %100 correct files to trigger helm module I think, we should find some of them.",
